{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension compared\ncase-insensitively. A source with any syntax error MUST fail to parse, reporting\nthe line of its first error. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other\nnodes MUST be transparent, so their outline descendants appear under the\nnearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the sources satisfy `requirements.references.outlines`.**\n\n**Implementation, obligation by obligation**\n\n- **Pinned grammars and extension selection.**\n  - `Cargo.toml` pins `tree-sitter` and every grammar crate exactly (`=0.27.1`, `=0.25.0`, `=0.24.2`, and so on).\n  - `profiles()` in `src/outline/profile.rs` lists the Rust, Python, TypeScript, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML profiles.\n  - `profile_for_path` lowercases the extension before matching. Anything else, including no extension, falls back to the plain-text profile in `src/outline/plain.rs`.\n- **Syntax errors.**\n  - `parse_redacted` in `src/outline/mod.rs` calls `check_errors`. It walks the tree in source order and fails on the first error or missing node, reporting that node's line through `OutlineError`.\n  - `OutlineCache::parse` in `src/inputs.rs` and the `outline` tool in `src/authoring/tools.rs` surface the error.\n- **Plain text.** `plain_text` creates one anonymous `line` node per non-blank line and skips blank lines.\n- **Profiles and naming.**\n  - Each profile has a `KindRule` table in its own file (`rust.rs`, `python.rs`, `typescript.rs`, `css.rs`, `markdown.rs`, `toml.rs`, `yaml.rs`).\n  - Each row maps a grammar kind to a short outline kind plus a `NameRule`: `Anonymous`, `Field`, `FirstChild` or `Custom`. This states whether a node is named and by which field or child.\n- **Transparency and root.**\n  - `Builder::walk` treats unmapped nodes as transparent, so their outline descendants attach to the nearest outline ancestor.\n  - `root_node` always creates the root (index 0). Unmapped top-level source belongs to it, and the code introduces no fallback kinds.\n- **Attached siblings.** The Rust profile attaches attributes and outer doc comments, and the Python profile attaches decorators. `finish` includes them in the node's line range, and the structural hash includes them.\n- **Comments.** `is_comment` excludes comments from `maps`, so they are never outline nodes.\n- **Documentation.** `md/reference.md`, section \"Outlines\" and \"Outline profiles\", has a full table of profiles, extensions, kinds, grammar nodes and names. It also covers the per-profile details and says grammar versions are pinned and upgrades are deliberate.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `src/outline/tests.rs` has a test for each profile: Python, TypeScript and TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML. The Rust selector tests cover Rust.\n- `reports_parse_failures_with_lines` covers a Rust error on line 2 and a Python error. It also covers a TOML source with several errors, checking that the first one (line 3) is reported and that the message format is `line N: ...`.\n- `profiles_are_selected_by_extension_case_insensitively` covers upper-case extensions, no extension, and `notes.rs.txt`.\n- `plain_text_lines_are_interchangeable_anonymous_nodes` covers blank lines being skipped and the `line` kind.\n- `file_level_inner_doc_comments_and_unmapped_code_belong_to_the_root` and `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds` check root membership and the absence of fallback kinds. They use unmapped Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML constructs.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs` has an end-to-end `outline` tool test. It checks the nested structure, that comments are absent, that plain text works, that a missing file is rejected, and that a denied file is refused.\n- `src/redaction/tests.rs` checks that redaction never changes an outline.\n- The `comment_*` tests check that comments are excluded from outline nodes.\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps)**\n\n- Rust's `impl_name`, `extern_name`, `item_macro` and `in_item_list` in `src/outline/rust.rs` carry `{impl}` for `rust_item_selectors` and `add_input_item`, not for `outlines`. They are Rust-specific naming and mapping details, and the Rust `KINDS` table and `PROFILE` that use them are marked `{impl}` for `outlines`.\n- The markdown `is_comment` is marked only for `outline_comments`, which is the requirement that governs it.\n- Syntax-error tests exercise only the Rust, Python and TOML grammars. The error path is generic code shared by all grammars, so this is sufficient.\n\nImplementation and tests together cover every obligation, and I found no unmarked behaviour that the requirement doesn't specify.",
  "references": "5c3d8f704564150e65bae6871877b34c1b1f616732ab43f73aadad2ebae3e041",
